} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209d435-198d-515f-b8b4-60c9562f6bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269560} {:answer/id #uuid "273798d5-f257-5699-8aa9-4f4c2f307f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269613} {:answer/id #uuid "3692e43f-c9b5-5ffd-8fc6-a562924ff89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269764} {:answer/id #uuid "7a671224-3dde-5038-92af-e145da2474b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270352} {:answer/id #uuid "8e191cef-1fcc-5bb8-9ab2-aa4afff1bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e191cef-1fcc-5bb8-9ab2-aa4afff1bdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270499} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9eedf2-2e58-52db-8e06-a9079bb28565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271567}] :question/id #uuid "b33c0ce6-8797-5117-a675-fc9bb0069eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-141552" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-11x+25.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a7ce45-8f89-5292-a9f7-83588fa540d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6a7ce45-8f89-5292-a9f7-83588fa540d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289351} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bfdb33f-3e5a-560d-b881-280805ddbed2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdfeff32-20d1-5a8c-9bf3-84db4cf46043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfeff32-20d1-5a8c-9bf3-84db4cf46043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289406} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc602a12-4abd-5560-9d31-a86b4eba380b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290174}] :question/id #uuid "71bc1100-5cb2-564e-9a6b-89a542c6c7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-223639" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-11x+30.$\n\n${x}^{2}-11x+30=(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}-$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3266af3-d507-5677-9cca-fe953b9f4291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3266af3-d507-5677-9cca-fe953b9f4291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d49c639-c1b4-5d1c-988b-463effd88b79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a2a9da-d25b-54f5-866a-796316d4daa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0a2a9da-d25b-54f5-866a-796316d4daa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffba8f81-6bad-5095-8d2a-7a84517eadec" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290255}] :question/id #uuid "b072df2c-3382-5adf-80f6-f89bc73cbee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-223640" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${w}^{2}-3w+3.$\n\n${w}^{2}-3w+3=(w\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+3\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"} :step/id #uuid "5c579b82-a690-58c4-a13a-883a3fdebaae"}] :activity/title "Completing the Square With Odd Linear Terms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184426}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050222 :topic/id #uuid "37687988-4a1b-507d-bbaa-9d93da76b9a6" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184083 :question/id #uuid "6e12005d-4652-532c-a715-3ab9d9ad3618" :question/math-academy-id "e-7139" :question/problem "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-3x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\dfrac32$ and $q=\\dfrac{11}{4}.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef3962e-a6bb-5a67-a558-58cd427de315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505964} {:answer/id #uuid "33d18b14-4374-57fc-bccd-ad6999325131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506002} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d4b756-0892-5fb7-92ca-8db3da31c1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506240} {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb226441-7167-55d6-90f6-42c6f33cce44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} :answer-field/id #uuid "6773a66b-b95a-576d-aff0-70a25d61a27e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183487}] :question/id #uuid "d367cd34-05c6-5d03-a048-21bce9745fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-60351" :question/problem "The equation $5{x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}x) & =3 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 5[{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $q=\\frac{16}{5}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{15}{5}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4729e5c5-d121-5071-90ac-905326cae80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403087} {:answer/id #uuid "148ab9f1-4a75-549b-80f9-e24ca927be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403088} {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32de8d-323c-57e3-a4c6-10f2b23289bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403090} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7de99-c75b-55c2-bfe2-679645b43eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090f79-b14c-5148-89c6-fad864772e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403086}] :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07967e46-3d53-5076-933f-f82b5f556405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244375} {:answer/id #uuid "401d3db9-46c6-5718-9d5d-157bd289930b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244875} {:answer/id #uuid "ced9c051-dab3-5f30-85db-fb6e73c83a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{25}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246162} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4131a8-2bd5-5dc8-8936-3564d8e553a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246419} {:answer/id #uuid "f25b295c-3860-584d-b014-b08e0d6fae63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee4131a8-2bd5-5dc8-8936-3564d8e553a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246419} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c061393-35f4-5189-baec-26f62e17b0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246820}] :question/id #uuid "165c4c89-9a6c-555e-8270-074787fb6a50" :question/math-academy-id "q-112589" :question/problem "The equation $6{z}^{2}+10z-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{2}+10z-1 & =0 \\\\ 6{z}^{2}+10z & =1 \\\\ 6({z}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}z) & =1 \\\\ 6[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{36}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{36} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{6} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} & =\\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{6}$ and $q=\\frac{31}{6}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{31}{6}=\\frac{36}{6}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92714716-5bbe-5cee-94c5-d4a5659903a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{11}{4},\\,z=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264254} {:answer/id #uuid "a5141912-69d1-5f4a-bca0-9d7248df1949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264411} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa13dc-c910-5b24-a8c9-a978ec6e8a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264761} {:answer/id #uuid "e33d3856-1005-5557-b459-f0f8f8c83489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{13}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264974} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b99594-5bf2-5660-9213-d4ace307273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{17}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa13dc-c910-5b24-a8c9-a978ec6e8a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ab3eaf-e5a0-5af1-8071-252570846a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265570}] :question/id #uuid "e3fdc752-039f-5f05-8441-586cbafa095b" :question/math-academy-id "q-12983" :question/problem "The equation $4{z}^{2}+4z=12$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+4z & =12 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+z) & =12 \\\\ {z}^{2}+z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=\\frac{1}{2},q=\\frac{13}{4},$ and $p+q=\\frac{15}{4}.$ \n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\ |z+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023e85b9-b324-5fae-8b24-a993cf078c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=6,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269286} {:answer/id #uuid "051cc304-af83-50b4-a01c-91d408eee4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269314} {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec254ead-7c72-506d-8d45-a216984be5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271344} {:answer/id #uuid "f2794853-d747-5b28-8e9f-17e9eaced79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b14b80a-26a2-5d34-a91a-5c29354c2e02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271702}] :question/id #uuid "948cfe82-2b59-583b-87a6-8211c3b5ce23" :question/math-academy-id "q-141627" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-15=12z$ can be written in the form $(z-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-15 & =12z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-12z & =15 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-4z) & =15 \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=2$ and $q=9.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |z-2| & =3 \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07de0bc5-2e03-55c1-8235-b99685231d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-6,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315158} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa55fc8-8e01-5d9f-a0ed-4877d9ac9b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315484} {:answer/id #uuid "8db534ef-9234-59fa-9366-f7d77532c129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315911} {:answer/id #uuid "96a75349-c57e-5994-86f1-10108329bfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315956} {:answer/id #uuid "c79bf9c0-638f-55f8-922c-dfd1f252f70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a75349-c57e-5994-86f1-10108329bfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315956} :answer-field/id #uuid "68589f89-559c-52ae-89aa-4fd6fe5fa086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316796}] :question/id #uuid "de3b2a31-7290-5ee6-bd88-a474c5c4972e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3431" :question/problem "The equation $2{z}^{2}+4z=10$  can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}+q=0.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}+4z & =10 \\\\ 2({z}^{2}+2z) & =10 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-1 & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, $p=1$ and $q=-6.$ \n\nNow, let's solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1)^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ |z+1| & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "726a10dd-feaf-5f7f-b835-dec3a07ae7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330978} {:answer/id #uuid "74b6b76a-9550-52f9-a4d3-2f800679b6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c505252-91d1-590a-808b-3cf1c17b9435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331042} {:answer/id #uuid "9a443997-a7c7-50d4-b7b4-11f0027f3ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331335} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0f296f-4d41-5ba7-932b-22e6da52139b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b6b76a-9550-52f9-a4d3-2f800679b6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330998} :answer-field/id #uuid "8042aed5-3c76-5e6f-8576-8ece7bbb17ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332430}] :question/id #uuid "fa441664-1ddd-591f-b1d7-6cdcd31b5094" :question/math-academy-id "q-60352" :question/problem "The equation $3{x}^{2}-4x+1=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}x) & =-1 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}x+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 3[{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{9} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2361c934-4058-589e-ba90-c9c33d22f025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330254} {:answer/id #uuid "8c159308-4370-5e87-9d87-93cf52b8dd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331193} {:answer/id #uuid "d00b0b38-c38e-5feb-9322-1bbafd7b879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331793} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b902a-d3e2-5bfb-990d-2760797a15c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-4$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332049} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8562d3-6502-5356-91c0-b34bf4f6a4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-2$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c159308-4370-5e87-9d87-93cf52b8dd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331193} :answer-field/id #uuid "e687e8df-bbd0-5576-a13d-8ab874669a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332605}] :question/id #uuid "5e573ffc-662c-53c4-981b-83f42066665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60359" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}+10x+12=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side,  factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+5x) & =-12 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}] & =-12 \\\\ 2[{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}] & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{2} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =-12+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{5}{2}| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+\\frac{5}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15778872-121f-5b94-8744-9a4c9cf32f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330121} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3dfa53-4d0c-5bb3-9a4c-bd1ad30b1391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330350} {:answer/id #uuid "759b0a96-8ac6-5393-b1ad-e344cf880c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331006} {:answer/id #uuid "a18fbe33-0cd3-5a4b-9fb0-3720b35c2568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331403} {:answer/id #uuid "ba870abb-664c-54e7-a9c0-fba55234163f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba870abb-664c-54e7-a9c0-fba55234163f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331596} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e6e669-7eab-57c6-99be-d7d165ed2a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332365}] :question/id #uuid "f103c196-548b-5d1f-b943-377277db1202" :question/math-academy-id "q-60361" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}-6x-3=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}x] & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 4[{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b361b7f6-2975-5ed2-b3d6-cb981bcbc5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399762} {:answer/id #uuid "af436aca-c25b-50d1-ae25-749ecc05a446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4e5843-97cc-5c3b-b643-9e3130891ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399764} {:answer/id #uuid "04e15661-c522-5a93-a20e-dafb0bf87524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f1bfdb-7442-5063-835b-afe41546d21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399761}] :question/id #uuid "eaa71aa3-e92b-5db0-96a6-7043b42bf47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112524" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =5 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc9c0e4-94e6-5a58-b402-1569ccdf0546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506114} {:answer/id #uuid "854c086c-10d6-59fc-a728-734ed635b82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=-\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506144} {:answer/id #uuid "9dedfe61-c595-5e0a-ab8d-634757cce0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{25}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506189} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b96d21-d357-5674-892a-42feaf9e8b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{3}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356} :answer-field/id #uuid "9418bd8b-7dee-502f-b277-9872940fd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506447}] :question/id #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35049" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135} :answer-field/id #uuid "6292167a-5bc6-5c4a-8390-f09eba2cfd71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81580ec-b17d-59a4-8b79-db833bfb273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506503}] :question/id #uuid "6f9c9fb5-398b-5a72-983c-e3a77bff1e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-218773" :question/problem "By completing the square, the equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+8=0$ is equivalent to\n\n$\\,\\,3(x\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+12x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =-8 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4x) & =-8 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & -8 \\\\ 3[{(x+2)}^{2}-4] & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+2)}^{2}-3\\cdot 4 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2}-12 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =-8+12 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184084 :question/id #uuid "9dfe0c3a-2c54-5270-ab0d-0d2dad09a199" :question/math-academy-id "e-7140" :question/problem "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+2x+1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-x) & =1 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\dfrac12$ and $q=\\dfrac32.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30938dcd-6958-5d27-99ab-ac151ecf0f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505998} {:answer/id #uuid "51247d6a-6677-500b-b718-1c064593b669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506048} {:answer/id #uuid "83912612-984f-5701-9ae3-68c22057a433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506141} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} {:answer/id #uuid "bca3e5a5-72f7-53a4-98d8-5a74a18fa611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a373b5f-ae93-59e6-ae47-3ffe140f606a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183493}] :question/id #uuid "219e2398-5583-50ea-9262-64c35b6b5639" :question/math-academy-id "q-60357" :question/problem "The equation $-5{x}^{2}+5x-1=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-5{x}^{2}+5x-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x & =-1 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-x) & =-1 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 5[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd761c6-5e4f-5dd4-8158-b400ce617cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-10, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183506} {:answer/id #uuid "21585eea-89d9-5aef-ae00-5fe75b7d3d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 1, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183507} {:answer/id #uuid "3204af01-f6c3-5ae4-88a7-b734f319f5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183508} {:answer/id #uuid "458c2c87-71b6-5330-b0e5-ea7c4d01b683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183509} {:answer/id #uuid "58018827-c6cc-556e-9392-fb39618bb430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183510}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf3ff66-e64d-5a0a-8eb7-b50dfa984489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183505}] :question/id #uuid "559b76f2-38be-5715-a95e-544cb0c73acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-60365" :question/problem "The equation $-3w^{2} - 6w + 7 = 0$ can be written in the form $3(w + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048c370a-d997-5ff0-ba16-7cff9bdbe50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244353} {:answer/id #uuid "0490f1f3-4831-57e4-bcf6-2ae18f95d615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=-1$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244354} {:answer/id #uuid "1063c73e-00a0-59f7-901b-32560b73a160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466} {:answer/id #uuid "253a82e2-d2d2-56f9-8b0f-f2908ecb46b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244644} {:answer/id #uuid "92d1a4ef-69ff-51b9-8604-8d0d12324e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=-\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1063c73e-00a0-59f7-901b-32560b73a160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466} :answer-field/id #uuid "a251d990-7bf3-575f-b894-6076d932cbe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246834}] :question/id #uuid "bdfe00ea-c67d-5b81-860c-24f0330009dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112605" :question/problem "The equation $-6{t}^{2}+5t-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(t-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-6{t}^{2}+5t-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t & =-1 \\\\ 6({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{6}t) & =-1 \\\\ 6[{t}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{12}t+{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 6[{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{144}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{144} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{24} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{25}{24} \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{144}} \\\\ |t-\\frac{5}{12}| & =\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t-\\frac{5}{12} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}\\pm \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f2276b-e8a8-540f-8b09-ad2c17e8a222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-4,\\,x=-3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245039} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d40120-9eb2-5058-a6df-63b726534b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245815} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fbe0f9-f9d9-5ebd-9f55-c0d2e274cf3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-6\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245960} {:answer/id #uuid "c182de89-7c61-594d-8578-876ef8e0d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246039} {:answer/id #uuid "e42136d0-93eb-5086-b3b6-6eda7d538a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-4,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c182de89-7c61-594d-8578-876ef8e0d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246039} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1bba4e-b837-5729-aedd-ad4396316e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246763}] :question/id #uuid "5d5a2dc1-b091-5d17-b744-bab152c4767b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112607" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-32x-61=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-32x-61) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x & =-61 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+8x) & =-61 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-61 \\\\ 4[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 16 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2}-64 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =-61+64 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=3.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2e916e-820e-5711-be0e-2c1c76a3fde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=1,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244548} {:answer/id #uuid "3b748e4b-d938-588b-b076-bbcd8d75aa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244837} {:answer/id #uuid "a04ed1b4-42e2-574d-94a9-63c58d31a418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245738} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8ca059-01f7-59da-a8b5-591cc3e1b817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=1,\\,w=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $w=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245979} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf92a60-cfde-56b2-9d25-7077429c9c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b748e4b-d938-588b-b076-bbcd8d75aa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b35a41e-de27-5d1a-9186-968191078e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246765}] :question/id #uuid "7a14e1ad-9f79-57f9-93d0-d6d12002fa7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112625" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-16w+9=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-16w+9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w & =9 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+4w) & =9 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot w+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 4[{(w+2)}^{2}-4] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2}-16 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w+2| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w+2 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =-2\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =-2-\\frac{5}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ w & =-2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583} :answer-field/id #uuid "755bfc03-bd02-52ab-8aab-d56ea4d74101" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf4fcaa-8406-516c-8a6c-82c8165c0bcf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c37bd49-077a-53a1-a295-f2ea33481465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272} :answer-field/id #uuid "e031303d-1afc-5f05-b049-a9f58c47689f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290208}] :question/id #uuid "94af16d4-6673-5335-b83a-58895c41f826" :question/math-academy-id "q-218774" :question/problem "$$\n-4{x}^{2}+16x-11=0\n$$\n\nBy completing the square, the above equation is equivalent to$\\,\\,4(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}, and the solutions are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}+16x-11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x) & =-11 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[{(x-2)}^{2}-4] & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2}-16 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =16-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(x-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d4ff1d-17d3-5fe8-82b1-ec81d2e6b17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351916} {:answer/id #uuid "4520dbf5-6afd-5a3d-b6dc-507c17fd7bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352207} {:answer/id #uuid "64c3e65f-4b44-5f05-aa82-4946f2668a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459} {:answer/id #uuid "731ffe8d-7b74-54a0-93de-44437a0dd9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352575} {:answer/id #uuid "911b28fa-0fdf-5df7-9c0a-e16f45defc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c3e65f-4b44-5f05-aa82-4946f2668a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459} :answer-field/id #uuid "4584bfbe-de8f-5441-9ac4-1419da6b0c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353936}] :question/id #uuid "1351d7ed-3243-5cce-931b-f3bf665c5c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-86412" :question/problem "The equation $-2{x}^{2}+12x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+12x-9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x & =-9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-6x) & =-9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-9 \\\\ 2[{(x-3)}^{2}-9] & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 9 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2}-18 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =-9+18 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51235c4b-9ea8-5be6-9202-8faae512d7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399769} {:answer/id #uuid "18c50e74-54e2-5b08-8781-bdac4e3eb829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399770} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef860dc-5a1c-5a5d-8cba-4a4b862a9b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399771} {:answer/id #uuid "c6502671-64f9-549c-ad24-6af57af49c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399772} {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773} :answer-field/id #uuid "132377e4-a98b-5f45-bb48-4f5451b52524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399768}] :question/id #uuid "b0945056-0521-55d9-80fa-5db83ff53407" :question/math-academy-id "q-112612" :question/problem "The equation $-4{z}^{2}-16z-7=0$ can be written in the form $4(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{z}^{2}-16z-7) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z & =-7 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+4z) & =-7 \\\\ 4[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-7 \\\\ 4[{(z+2)}^{2}-4] & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =-7+16 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(z+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |z+2| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5408298f-7273-5f8e-b08d-eb9052a72e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403074} {:answer/id #uuid "55c297c3-ce19-541b-8f15-0e1cff269acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403075} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc7cd2a-4140-5747-8785-3c2be9dddaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403077} {:answer/id #uuid "ef782508-1876-5861-8688-45bcea667f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} :answer-field/id #uuid "838eec31-f001-5f3e-95ff-aee58026f6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403073}] :question/id #uuid "a1ac9dcc-ee5b-5438-8680-0a3c7af8b759" :question/math-academy-id "q-60145" :question/problem "The equation $-20{x}^{2}+60x-41=0$ can be written in the form $20(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-20{x}^{2}+60x-41) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x & =-41 \\\\ 20({x}^{2}-3x) & =-41 \\\\ 20[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}] & =-41 \\\\ 20[{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}] & =-41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-20\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-45 & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =45-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3561cc-7a36-53c3-8463-a08b60f2e422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403081} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0c8fbf-a096-54ef-a916-0cf279874a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403082} {:answer/id #uuid "84b429b9-e24f-5b71-bb06-7a7076b1bd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403083} {:answer/id #uuid "c808c746-284d-54ac-ab48-93af25059dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403084} {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085} :answer-field/id #uuid "5281993a-3636-5f8e-a209-1d71b7850d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403080}] :question/id #uuid "a7884f70-6bc5-5884-9b9e-474178b4363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60356" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}-2x+6=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-2x+6) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x] & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}] & =6 \\\\ 3[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =6+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{3}$, $q=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404334} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330}] :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a96b493-4dc4-5e3d-affb-5104b074be81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404338} {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404339} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3d04fe-ed8a-51ec-a836-0b919705ce38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404340} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6ab5a-4ea6-5f88-bef2-511b7baac0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404341} {:answer/id #uuid "f625d56d-5576-52e1-a8c2-d2b4aa1f2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404339} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a12275-0df5-56dd-8578-50b10f58f3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404337}] :question/id #uuid "e1734459-a432-53d0-859c-83686d8f7d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-86414" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}+36x-94=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}+36x-94) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-12x] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{(x-6)}^{2}-36] & =-94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-6)}^{2}-3\\cdot 36 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2}-108 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =-94+108 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-6$, $q=14.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=14-(-6)=14+6=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344}] :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d50330c-784c-5a84-ac1e-5c7c5be92530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505935} {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} {:answer/id #uuid "8910933f-a80b-5b82-961d-2627b5f5280c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506150} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee5ca9-ea89-54cd-839a-29262cd0a71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506213} {:answer/id #uuid "f62dae0d-aa09-5a1e-b79c-fc4344431af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb7d856-8042-5bbe-be63-7001e1b25a2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506486}] :question/id #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" :question/math-academy-id "q-60363" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516119} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8dd15f-6ef6-5465-ba84-3a0eaa094c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516135} {:answer/id #uuid "b903bd14-e04d-5fe0-bd6c-e730be650761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516497} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cfb96e-8f1f-5a2a-8263-292cf5833e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516526} {:answer/id #uuid "d197f0c1-c269-5113-87f5-a4378c002223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-6\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516119} :answer-field/id #uuid "53de4293-1518-51e6-b385-eaf97b7abfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516684}] :question/id #uuid "b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385" :question/math-academy-id "q-112614" :question/problem "The equation $-2{x}^{2}-16x-23=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}-16x-23) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x & =-23 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+8x) & =-23 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-23 \\\\ 2[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2}-2\\cdot 16 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2}-32 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =-23+32 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=9.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183914 :question/id #uuid "9815dede-5c47-5430-a58a-218313f78412" :question/math-academy-id "e-10014" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-4x & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+(-2)(-1) & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2-2 & =1-2 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)} \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90643212-3c8b-5139-8d76-d0299f05eeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181189} {:answer/id #uuid "e50f2ba4-cc94-5848-94b0-86acc67cbf56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1 ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181190} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1565c8-c84b-5e60-9caa-1122ee1b0e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2 ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181191} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbd3902-b6db-59a5-877a-a1fd71cece57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2 ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181192} {:answer/id #uuid "73d53c14-0eac-596a-938b-aa05de2f99e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2 ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181193}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a598262b-5057-5238-9c74-c3bb11735720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181188}] :question/id #uuid "82551fb5-ff0f-5087-b173-0f8c27ff3264" :question/math-academy-id "q-141818" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-3x^{2} - 12x = 8$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^{2} + 4x + 4$ is a perfect square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b391957b-173e-5547-8ca6-ed6371710d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183110} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ec3446-1ab2-5be6-96f1-014eea744e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183111} {:answer/id #uuid "2825095f-104f-5893-8c2d-7eb0d332624b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183112} {:answer/id #uuid "37fc8256-e14e-5f04-a279-22cbdb6580b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183113} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7f01fd-d721-57b0-bf86-e238f2673c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183114}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5dec1112-f4db-5f12-bb19-77df8252978e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183109}] :question/id #uuid "516de231-477e-5130-ac4e-2901359e5b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-3432" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3y^{2} - 12y = 4$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $y^{2} - 4y + 4$ is a perfect square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241b1404-c2aa-575d-8065-ec106981f5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269585} {:answer/id #uuid "7919a728-b543-536d-8054-0a184713c2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270341} {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} {:answer/id #uuid "e3105ac8-37d5-5739-abc9-4ef7c2fc18d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271267} {:answer/id #uuid "f25bf37e-9c95-5124-b37f-32e6744d69d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe18474-5ded-5269-8f07-0ba5488b85e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271944}] :question/id #uuid "7c3581b3-19ec-58f3-879c-94addceac5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141817" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5{y}^{2}-40y=-76.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-8y+16$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+16=(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-40y & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-8y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}) & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-16] & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $80$ to both sides and then dividing by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80+80 & =-76+80 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ |y-4| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ba4407-03eb-51b1-b004-b940f852c317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269265} {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} {:answer/id #uuid "3a359fbb-bf86-5e11-909d-f780a241551a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269799} {:answer/id #uuid "ad957086-768b-5537-8625-1aa43a2bf2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270814} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0efdd1-5309-518f-b349-e728470eb25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} :answer-field/id #uuid "f092d464-9988-5471-9683-fb59377b140b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271931}] :question/id #uuid "03b9f129-6f00-5dcf-9f10-c4784f08b856" :question/math-academy-id "q-141824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-6{y}^{2}+24y=21.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-4y+4$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{y}^{2}+24y & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-4y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+(-6)(-4) & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $24$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24-24 & =21-24 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-6)} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c2ed7-3b4a-5f80-9ab8-6d2264b502c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269637} {:answer/id #uuid "5afc9e05-2f19-56d4-8fd8-4dd154cab168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270079} {:answer/id #uuid "911deb27-d995-5dea-8e24-824959937b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270530} {:answer/id #uuid "b53b11d7-ba13-5f62-b662-6e3b9d578056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270880} {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e5bd75-2087-50a4-836f-a79a60347260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271635}] :question/id #uuid "a2ec4003-7bb2-5f7b-b2d0-ca23e28bb9c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141826" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-7{y}^{2}-42y=62.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}+6y+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+6y+9=(y+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{y}^{2}-42y & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+6y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+(-7)(-9) & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $63$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63-63 & =62-63 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-7)} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}} \\\\ |y+3| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d882d-3cd8-5591-95ab-4f02b364c9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269444} {:answer/id #uuid "27b5c406-c7ab-5ca1-8538-f5588287699a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269620} {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e265-47ef-51f2-a502-687b1a4fa602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270539} {:answer/id #uuid "f39b5916-e78a-5378-beb3-0cc0c23fcae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} :answer-field/id #uuid "05523259-00ab-5145-bcab-f2aec15fcd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271512}] :question/id #uuid "1044f32f-7390-525d-94d0-30c2ecd3e1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141829" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-4{x}^{2}+4x=-2.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}={(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $1$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-4),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1-1 & =-2-1 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c9dab48-f4ec-50d5-997f-66003dcb0d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} {:answer/id #uuid "78463560-21e0-5b25-866c-e90cd7340bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388844} {:answer/id #uuid "b9db4f34-08d3-52b7-b130-1953818d1cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388845} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcc5cac-e58c-525f-92e0-44013ae6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} :answer-field/id #uuid "32deee12-64c9-53b5-a191-c9595d854af8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388842}] :question/id #uuid "6cd3a3fd-0ec4-5982-bdba-cd236ee44004" :question/math-academy-id "q-141811" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}+24x=-11.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+6x+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+24x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+6x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $36$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36+36 & =-11+36 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a5c631-15ae-5872-945b-175674fabfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506221} {:answer/id #uuid "bd51cecd-6c04-520f-9fa4-54a69b142f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506252} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0938db-92c7-585b-8cbd-31116bc33d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506271} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e520c-9345-5603-92aa-5989d3118aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38d1eab-b846-57b1-8eca-3e79f6301a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506494}] :question/id #uuid "1724c4ea-89ed-51e2-a2bf-8d02f0f074fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-141806" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bb1ee1-44d2-5a8b-a0ff-8a61b2d8e549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd21be66-27be-55af-af71-359acfb6c6dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506507}] :question/id #uuid "cca814ca-cf90-5add-8be7-53a49b278718" :question/math-academy-id "q-218766" :question/problem "Solve the equation $9{x}^{2}-18x=7.$  Write the solutions in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*\n\n$\\,{x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-18x & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides and then dividing by $9$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9+9 & =7+9 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Completing the Square"} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185028} :activity/id #uuid "4d6dc1b1-bce9-51bb-afb1-2c7a2cf24e76" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184083 :question/id #uuid "6e12005d-4652-532c-a715-3ab9d9ad3618" :question/math-academy-id "e-7139" :question/problem "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-3x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\dfrac32$ and $q=\\dfrac{11}{4}.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef3962e-a6bb-5a67-a558-58cd427de315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505964} {:answer/id #uuid "33d18b14-4374-57fc-bccd-ad6999325131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506002} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d4b756-0892-5fb7-92ca-8db3da31c1e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506240} {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb226441-7167-55d6-90f6-42c6f33cce44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} :answer-field/id #uuid "6773a66b-b95a-576d-aff0-70a25d61a27e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183487}] :question/id #uuid "d367cd34-05c6-5d03-a048-21bce9745fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-60351" :question/problem "The equation $5{x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}x) & =3 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 5[{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $q=\\frac{16}{5}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{15}{5}=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4729e5c5-d121-5071-90ac-905326cae80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403087} {:answer/id #uuid "148ab9f1-4a75-549b-80f9-e24ca927be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403088} {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32de8d-323c-57e3-a4c6-10f2b23289bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403090} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7de99-c75b-55c2-bfe2-679645b43eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090f79-b14c-5148-89c6-fad864772e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403086}] :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07967e46-3d53-5076-933f-f82b5f556405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244375} {:answer/id #uuid "401d3db9-46c6-5718-9d5d-157bd289930b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244875} {:answer/id #uuid "ced9c051-dab3-5f30-85db-fb6e73c83a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{25}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246162} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4131a8-2bd5-5dc8-8936-3564d8e553a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246419} {:answer/id #uuid "f25b295c-3860-584d-b014-b08e0d6fae63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee4131a8-2bd5-5dc8-8936-3564d8e553a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246419} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c061393-35f4-5189-baec-26f62e17b0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246820}] :question/id #uuid "165c4c89-9a6c-555e-8270-074787fb6a50" :question/math-academy-id "q-112589" :question/problem "The equation $6{z}^{2}+10z-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{2}+10z-1 & =0 \\\\ 6{z}^{2}+10z & =1 \\\\ 6({z}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}z) & =1 \\\\ 6[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{36}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{36} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{6} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} & =\\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{6}$ and $q=\\frac{31}{6}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{31}{6}=\\frac{36}{6}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92714716-5bbe-5cee-94c5-d4a5659903a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{11}{4},\\,z=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264254} {:answer/id #uuid "a5141912-69d1-5f4a-bca0-9d7248df1949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264411} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa13dc-c910-5b24-a8c9-a978ec6e8a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264761} {:answer/id #uuid "e33d3856-1005-5557-b459-f0f8f8c83489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{13}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264974} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b99594-5bf2-5660-9213-d4ace307273e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{17}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa13dc-c910-5b24-a8c9-a978ec6e8a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=\\frac{15}{4},\\,z=\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264761} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ab3eaf-e5a0-5af1-8071-252570846a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265570}] :question/id #uuid "e3fdc752-039f-5f05-8441-586cbafa095b" :question/math-academy-id "q-12983" :question/problem "The equation $4{z}^{2}+4z=12$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+4z & =12 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+z) & =12 \\\\ {z}^{2}+z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot z & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=\\frac{1}{2},q=\\frac{13}{4},$ and $p+q=\\frac{15}{4}.$ \n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\ |z+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{-1\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023e85b9-b324-5fae-8b24-a993cf078c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=6,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269286} {:answer/id #uuid "051cc304-af83-50b4-a01c-91d408eee4c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269314} {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec254ead-7c72-506d-8d45-a216984be5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271344} {:answer/id #uuid "f2794853-d747-5b28-8e9f-17e9eaced79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b14b80a-26a2-5d34-a91a-5c29354c2e02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271702}] :question/id #uuid "948cfe82-2b59-583b-87a6-8211c3b5ce23" :question/math-academy-id "q-141627" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-15=12z$ can be written in the form $(z-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-15 & =12z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-12z & =15 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-4z) & =15 \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=2$ and $q=9.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |z-2| & =3 \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07de0bc5-2e03-55c1-8235-b99685231d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-6,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315158} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa55fc8-8e01-5d9f-a0ed-4877d9ac9b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315484} {:answer/id #uuid "8db534ef-9234-59fa-9366-f7d77532c129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315911} {:answer/id #uuid "96a75349-c57e-5994-86f1-10108329bfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315956} {:answer/id #uuid "c79bf9c0-638f-55f8-922c-dfd1f252f70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a75349-c57e-5994-86f1-10108329bfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1,\\,z=-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315956} :answer-field/id #uuid "68589f89-559c-52ae-89aa-4fd6fe5fa086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316796}] :question/id #uuid "de3b2a31-7290-5ee6-bd88-a474c5c4972e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3431" :question/problem "The equation $2{z}^{2}+4z=10$  can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}+q=0.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}+4z & =10 \\\\ 2({z}^{2}+2z) & =10 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-1 & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, $p=1$ and $q=-6.$ \n\nNow, let's solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1)^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ |z+1| & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "726a10dd-feaf-5f7f-b835-dec3a07ae7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330978} {:answer/id #uuid "74b6b76a-9550-52f9-a4d3-2f800679b6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330998} {:answer/id #uuid "7c505252-91d1-590a-808b-3cf1c17b9435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331042} {:answer/id #uuid "9a443997-a7c7-50d4-b7b4-11f0027f3ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331335} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0f296f-4d41-5ba7-932b-22e6da52139b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b6b76a-9550-52f9-a4d3-2f800679b6e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330998} :answer-field/id #uuid "8042aed5-3c76-5e6f-8576-8ece7bbb17ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332430}] :question/id #uuid "fa441664-1ddd-591f-b1d7-6cdcd31b5094" :question/math-academy-id "q-60352" :question/problem "The equation $3{x}^{2}-4x+1=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}x) & =-1 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}x+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 3[{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{9} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2361c934-4058-589e-ba90-c9c33d22f025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330254} {:answer/id #uuid "8c159308-4370-5e87-9d87-93cf52b8dd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331193} {:answer/id #uuid "d00b0b38-c38e-5feb-9322-1bbafd7b879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331793} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b902a-d3e2-5bfb-990d-2760797a15c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-4$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332049} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8562d3-6502-5356-91c0-b34bf4f6a4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=-2$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c159308-4370-5e87-9d87-93cf52b8dd67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{2},\\,x=-3$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331193} :answer-field/id #uuid "e687e8df-bbd0-5576-a13d-8ab874669a2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332605}] :question/id #uuid "5e573ffc-662c-53c4-981b-83f42066665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60359" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}+10x+12=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side,  factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+10x+12 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+10x & =-12 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+5x) & =-12 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}] & =-12 \\\\ 2[{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}] & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{2} & =-12 \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =-12+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ 2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{5}{2}| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+\\frac{5}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{6}{3}=-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15778872-121f-5b94-8744-9a4c9cf32f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330121} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3dfa53-4d0c-5bb3-9a4c-bd1ad30b1391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330350} {:answer/id #uuid "759b0a96-8ac6-5393-b1ad-e344cf880c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{21}{4},\\,x=\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331006} {:answer/id #uuid "a18fbe33-0cd3-5a4b-9fb0-3720b35c2568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331403} {:answer/id #uuid "ba870abb-664c-54e7-a9c0-fba55234163f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba870abb-664c-54e7-a9c0-fba55234163f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331596} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e6e669-7eab-57c6-99be-d7d165ed2a19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332365}] :question/id #uuid "f103c196-548b-5d1f-b943-377277db1202" :question/math-academy-id "q-60361" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}-6x-3=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-6x-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6x & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}x] & =3 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 4[{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =3 \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{16}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{21}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b361b7f6-2975-5ed2-b3d6-cb981bcbc5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399762} {:answer/id #uuid "af436aca-c25b-50d1-ae25-749ecc05a446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399763} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4e5843-97cc-5c3b-b643-9e3130891ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399764} {:answer/id #uuid "04e15661-c522-5a93-a20e-dafb0bf87524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f1bfdb-7442-5063-835b-afe41546d21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399761}] :question/id #uuid "eaa71aa3-e92b-5db0-96a6-7043b42bf47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112524" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =5 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc9c0e4-94e6-5a58-b402-1569ccdf0546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506114} {:answer/id #uuid "854c086c-10d6-59fc-a728-734ed635b82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=-\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506144} {:answer/id #uuid "9dedfe61-c595-5e0a-ab8d-634757cce0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{25}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506189} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b96d21-d357-5674-892a-42feaf9e8b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{3}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356} :answer-field/id #uuid "9418bd8b-7dee-502f-b277-9872940fd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506447}] :question/id #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35049" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135} :answer-field/id #uuid "6292167a-5bc6-5c4a-8390-f09eba2cfd71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81580ec-b17d-59a4-8b79-db833bfb273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506503}] :question/id #uuid "6f9c9fb5-398b-5a72-983c-e3a77bff1e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-218773" :question/problem "By completing the square, the equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+8=0$ is equivalent to\n\n$\\,\\,3(x\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+12x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =-8 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4x) & =-8 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & -8 \\\\ 3[{(x+2)}^{2}-4] & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+2)}^{2}-3\\cdot 4 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2}-12 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =-8+12 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "17d44589-7cb8-5aae-aebc-f094c5be519a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184084 :question/id #uuid "9dfe0c3a-2c54-5270-ab0d-0d2dad09a199" :question/math-academy-id "e-7140" :question/problem "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+2x+1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-x) & =1 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\dfrac12$ and $q=\\dfrac32.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30938dcd-6958-5d27-99ab-ac151ecf0f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505998} {:answer/id #uuid "51247d6a-6677-500b-b718-1c064593b669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506048} {:answer/id #uuid "83912612-984f-5701-9ae3-68c22057a433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506141} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} {:answer/id #uuid "bca3e5a5-72f7-53a4-98d8-5a74a18fa611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a373b5f-ae93-59e6-ae47-3ffe140f606a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183493}] :question/id #uuid "219e2398-5583-50ea-9262-64c35b6b5639" :question/math-academy-id "q-60357" :question/problem "The equation $-5{x}^{2}+5x-1=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-5{x}^{2}+5x-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x & =-1 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-x) & =-1 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 5[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd761c6-5e4f-5dd4-8158-b400ce617cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q =-10, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183506} {:answer/id #uuid "21585eea-89d9-5aef-ae00-5fe75b7d3d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 1, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183507} {:answer/id #uuid "3204af01-f6c3-5ae4-88a7-b734f319f5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w =-1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183508} {:answer/id #uuid "458c2c87-71b6-5330-b0e5-ea7c4d01b683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183509} {:answer/id #uuid "58018827-c6cc-556e-9392-fb39618bb430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q = 10, w = 1 ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183510}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf3ff66-e64d-5a0a-8eb7-b50dfa984489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183505}] :question/id #uuid "559b76f2-38be-5715-a95e-544cb0c73acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-60365" :question/problem "The equation $-3w^{2} - 6w + 7 = 0$ can be written in the form $3(w + p)^{2} = q$. Find $q$, and solve the equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048c370a-d997-5ff0-ba16-7cff9bdbe50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244353} {:answer/id #uuid "0490f1f3-4831-57e4-bcf6-2ae18f95d615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{3},\\,t=-1$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244354} {:answer/id #uuid "1063c73e-00a0-59f7-901b-32560b73a160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466} {:answer/id #uuid "253a82e2-d2d2-56f9-8b0f-f2908ecb46b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{1}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244644} {:answer/id #uuid "92d1a4ef-69ff-51b9-8604-8d0d12324e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=-\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1063c73e-00a0-59f7-901b-32560b73a160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{5}{12},\\,t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244466} :answer-field/id #uuid "a251d990-7bf3-575f-b894-6076d932cbe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246834}] :question/id #uuid "bdfe00ea-c67d-5b81-860c-24f0330009dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112605" :question/problem "The equation $-6{t}^{2}+5t-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(t-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-6{t}^{2}+5t-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t & =-1 \\\\ 6({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{6}t) & =-1 \\\\ 6[{t}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{12}t+{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 6[{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{144}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{144} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{24} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{25}{24} \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{144}} \\\\ |t-\\frac{5}{12}| & =\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t-\\frac{5}{12} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}\\pm \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f2276b-e8a8-540f-8b09-ad2c17e8a222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-4,\\,x=-3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245039} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d40120-9eb2-5058-a6df-63b726534b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245815} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fbe0f9-f9d9-5ebd-9f55-c0d2e274cf3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-6\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245960} {:answer/id #uuid "c182de89-7c61-594d-8578-876ef8e0d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246039} {:answer/id #uuid "e42136d0-93eb-5086-b3b6-6eda7d538a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-4,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c182de89-7c61-594d-8578-876ef8e0d1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=3,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246039} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1bba4e-b837-5729-aedd-ad4396316e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246763}] :question/id #uuid "5d5a2dc1-b091-5d17-b744-bab152c4767b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112607" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-32x-61=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-32x-61) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x & =-61 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+8x) & =-61 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-61 \\\\ 4[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 16 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2}-64 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =-61+64 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=3.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2e916e-820e-5711-be0e-2c1c76a3fde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=1,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244548} {:answer/id #uuid "3b748e4b-d938-588b-b076-bbcd8d75aa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244837} {:answer/id #uuid "a04ed1b4-42e2-574d-94a9-63c58d31a418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245738} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8ca059-01f7-59da-a8b5-591cc3e1b817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=1,\\,w=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $w=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245979} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf92a60-cfde-56b2-9d25-7077429c9c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b748e4b-d938-588b-b076-bbcd8d75aa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b35a41e-de27-5d1a-9186-968191078e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246765}] :question/id #uuid "7a14e1ad-9f79-57f9-93d0-d6d12002fa7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112625" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-16w+9=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-16w+9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w & =9 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+4w) & =9 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot w+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 4[{(w+2)}^{2}-4] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2}-16 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w+2| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w+2 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =-2\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =-2-\\frac{5}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ w & =-2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583} :answer-field/id #uuid "755bfc03-bd02-52ab-8aab-d56ea4d74101" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf4fcaa-8406-516c-8a6c-82c8165c0bcf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c37bd49-077a-53a1-a295-f2ea33481465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272} :answer-field/id #uuid "e031303d-1afc-5f05-b049-a9f58c47689f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290208}] :question/id #uuid "94af16d4-6673-5335-b83a-58895c41f826" :question/math-academy-id "q-218774" :question/problem "$$\n-4{x}^{2}+16x-11=0\n$$\n\nBy completing the square, the above equation is equivalent to$\\,\\,4(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}, and the solutions are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}+16x-11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x) & =-11 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[{(x-2)}^{2}-4] & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2}-16 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =16-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(x-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d4ff1d-17d3-5fe8-82b1-ec81d2e6b17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351916} {:answer/id #uuid "4520dbf5-6afd-5a3d-b6dc-507c17fd7bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352207} {:answer/id #uuid "64c3e65f-4b44-5f05-aa82-4946f2668a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459} {:answer/id #uuid "731ffe8d-7b74-54a0-93de-44437a0dd9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352575} {:answer/id #uuid "911b28fa-0fdf-5df7-9c0a-e16f45defc49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c3e65f-4b44-5f05-aa82-4946f2668a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352459} :answer-field/id #uuid "4584bfbe-de8f-5441-9ac4-1419da6b0c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353936}] :question/id #uuid "1351d7ed-3243-5cce-931b-f3bf665c5c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-86412" :question/problem "The equation $-2{x}^{2}+12x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+12x-9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-12x+9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x & =-9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-6x) & =-9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-9 \\\\ 2[{(x-3)}^{2}-9] & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 9 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2}-18 & =-9 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =-9+18 \\\\ 2{(x-3)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3$ and $q=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399767 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51235c4b-9ea8-5be6-9202-8faae512d7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399769} {:answer/id #uuid "18c50e74-54e2-5b08-8781-bdac4e3eb829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399770} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef860dc-5a1c-5a5d-8cba-4a4b862a9b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399771} {:answer/id #uuid "c6502671-64f9-549c-ad24-6af57af49c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399772} {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773} :answer-field/id #uuid "132377e4-a98b-5f45-bb48-4f5451b52524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399768}] :question/id #uuid "b0945056-0521-55d9-80fa-5db83ff53407" :question/math-academy-id "q-112612" :question/problem "The equation $-4{z}^{2}-16z-7=0$ can be written in the form $4(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{z}^{2}-16z-7) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z & =-7 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+4z) & =-7 \\\\ 4[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-7 \\\\ 4[{(z+2)}^{2}-4] & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =-7+16 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(z+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |z+2| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5408298f-7273-5f8e-b08d-eb9052a72e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403074} {:answer/id #uuid "55c297c3-ce19-541b-8f15-0e1cff269acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403075} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc7cd2a-4140-5747-8785-3c2be9dddaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403077} {:answer/id #uuid "ef782508-1876-5861-8688-45bcea667f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} :answer-field/id #uuid "838eec31-f001-5f3e-95ff-aee58026f6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403073}] :question/id #uuid "a1ac9dcc-ee5b-5438-8680-0a3c7af8b759" :question/math-academy-id "q-60145" :question/problem "The equation $-20{x}^{2}+60x-41=0$ can be written in the form $20(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-20{x}^{2}+60x-41) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x & =-41 \\\\ 20({x}^{2}-3x) & =-41 \\\\ 20[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}] & =-41 \\\\ 20[{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}] & =-41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-20\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-45 & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =45-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3561cc-7a36-53c3-8463-a08b60f2e422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403081} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0c8fbf-a096-54ef-a916-0cf279874a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403082} {:answer/id #uuid "84b429b9-e24f-5b71-bb06-7a7076b1bd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403083} {:answer/id #uuid "c808c746-284d-54ac-ab48-93af25059dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403084} {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085} :answer-field/id #uuid "5281993a-3636-5f8e-a209-1d71b7850d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403080}] :question/id #uuid "a7884f70-6bc5-5884-9b9e-474178b4363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60356" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}-2x+6=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-2x+6) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x] & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}] & =6 \\\\ 3[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =6+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{3}$, $q=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404334} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330}] :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a96b493-4dc4-5e3d-affb-5104b074be81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404338} {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404339} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3d04fe-ed8a-51ec-a836-0b919705ce38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404340} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6ab5a-4ea6-5f88-bef2-511b7baac0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404341} {:answer/id #uuid "f625d56d-5576-52e1-a8c2-d2b4aa1f2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404339} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a12275-0df5-56dd-8578-50b10f58f3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404337}] :question/id #uuid "e1734459-a432-53d0-859c-83686d8f7d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-86414" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}+36x-94=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}+36x-94) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-12x] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{(x-6)}^{2}-36] & =-94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-6)}^{2}-3\\cdot 36 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2}-108 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =-94+108 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-6$, $q=14.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=14-(-6)=14+6=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344}] :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d50330c-784c-5a84-ac1e-5c7c5be92530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505935} {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} {:answer/id #uuid "8910933f-a80b-5b82-961d-2627b5f5280c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506150} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee5ca9-ea89-54cd-839a-29262cd0a71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506213} {:answer/id #uuid "f62dae0d-aa09-5a1e-b79c-fc4344431af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb7d856-8042-5bbe-be63-7001e1b25a2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506486}] :question/id #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" :question/math-academy-id "q-60363" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516119} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8dd15f-6ef6-5465-ba84-3a0eaa094c6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516135} {:answer/id #uuid "b903bd14-e04d-5fe0-bd6c-e730be650761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516497} {:answer/id #uuid "c3cfb96e-8f1f-5a2a-8263-292cf5833e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516526} {:answer/id #uuid "d197f0c1-c269-5113-87f5-a4378c002223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-6\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516119} :answer-field/id #uuid "53de4293-1518-51e6-b385-eaf97b7abfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516684}] :question/id #uuid "b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385" :question/math-academy-id "q-112614" :question/problem "The equation $-2{x}^{2}-16x-23=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}-16x-23) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x & =-23 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+8x) & =-23 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-23 \\\\ 2[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2}-2\\cdot 16 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2}-32 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =-23+32 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=9.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "ae30298b-d3cf-50b0-8100-8d0fc1d6a38b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185352 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of completing the square to solve quadratic equations with leading coefficients. \n\nAs an example, let's solve the following quadratic equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n4x^2-8x+3 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x+3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-3 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =-3 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding  $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =-3 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =-3+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac12$ and $x=\\dfrac32.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7d202804-803f-5139-b32a-fca689609abb" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9c761f1-c1eb-55db-b51b-2f4b7c894767" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183914 :question/id #uuid "9815dede-5c47-5430-a58a-218313f78412" :question/math-academy-id "e-10014" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-4x & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+(-2)(-1) & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2-2 & =1-2 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)} \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90643212-3c8b-5139-8d76-d0299f05eeac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181189} {:answer/id #uuid "e50f2ba4-cc94-5848-94b0-86acc67cbf56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1 ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181190} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1565c8-c84b-5e60-9caa-1122ee1b0e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2 ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181191} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbd3902-b6db-59a5-877a-a1fd71cece57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 2 ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181192} {:answer/id #uuid "73d53c14-0eac-596a-938b-aa05de2f99e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2 ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181193}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a598262b-5057-5238-9c74-c3bb11735720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181188}] :question/id #uuid "82551fb5-ff0f-5087-b173-0f8c27ff3264" :question/math-academy-id "q-141818" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-3x^{2} - 12x = 8$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^{2} + 4x + 4$ is a perfect square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b391957b-173e-5547-8ca6-ed6371710d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183110} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ec3446-1ab2-5be6-96f1-014eea744e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183111} {:answer/id #uuid "2825095f-104f-5893-8c2d-7eb0d332624b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183112} {:answer/id #uuid "37fc8256-e14e-5f04-a279-22cbdb6580b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2 ± \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183113} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7f01fd-d721-57b0-bf86-e238f2673c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2 ± \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183114}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5dec1112-f4db-5f12-bb19-77df8252978e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183109}] :question/id #uuid "516de231-477e-5130-ac4e-2901359e5b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-3432" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3y^{2} - 12y = 4$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $y^{2} - 4y + 4$ is a perfect square.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241b1404-c2aa-575d-8065-ec106981f5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269585} {:answer/id #uuid "7919a728-b543-536d-8054-0a184713c2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270341} {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} {:answer/id #uuid "e3105ac8-37d5-5739-abc9-4ef7c2fc18d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271267} {:answer/id #uuid "f25bf37e-9c95-5124-b37f-32e6744d69d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c82102a0-f740-5514-9212-0b3cd8c1d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271031} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe18474-5ded-5269-8f07-0ba5488b85e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271944}] :question/id #uuid "7c3581b3-19ec-58f3-879c-94addceac5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141817" :question/problem "Solve the equation $5{y}^{2}-40y=-76.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-8y+16$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+16=(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-40y & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-8y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y) & =-76 \\\\ 5({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}) & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-76 \\\\ 5[(y-4)^{2}-16] & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $80$ to both sides and then dividing by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(y-4)^{2}-80 & =-76 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2}-80+80 & =-76+80 \\\\ 5(y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\ |y-4| & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ba4407-03eb-51b1-b004-b940f852c317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269265} {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} {:answer/id #uuid "3a359fbb-bf86-5e11-909d-f780a241551a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269799} {:answer/id #uuid "ad957086-768b-5537-8625-1aa43a2bf2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270814} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0efdd1-5309-518f-b349-e728470eb25e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} :answer-field/id #uuid "f092d464-9988-5471-9683-fb59377b140b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271931}] :question/id #uuid "03b9f129-6f00-5dcf-9f10-c4784f08b856" :question/math-academy-id "q-141824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-6{y}^{2}+24y=21.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-4y+4$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{y}^{2}+24y & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-4y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+(-6)(-4) & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $24$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24-24 & =21-24 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-6)} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c2ed7-3b4a-5f80-9ab8-6d2264b502c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269637} {:answer/id #uuid "5afc9e05-2f19-56d4-8fd8-4dd154cab168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270079} {:answer/id #uuid "911deb27-d995-5dea-8e24-824959937b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270530} {:answer/id #uuid "b53b11d7-ba13-5f62-b662-6e3b9d578056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270880} {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e5bd75-2087-50a4-836f-a79a60347260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271635}] :question/id #uuid "a2ec4003-7bb2-5f7b-b2d0-ca23e28bb9c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141826" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-7{y}^{2}-42y=62.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}+6y+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+6y+9=(y+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{y}^{2}-42y & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+6y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+(-7)(-9) & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $63$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63-63 & =62-63 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-7)} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}} \\\\ |y+3| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d882d-3cd8-5591-95ab-4f02b364c9e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269444} {:answer/id #uuid "27b5c406-c7ab-5ca1-8538-f5588287699a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269620} {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e265-47ef-51f2-a502-687b1a4fa602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270539} {:answer/id #uuid "f39b5916-e78a-5378-beb3-0cc0c23fcae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} :answer-field/id #uuid "05523259-00ab-5145-bcab-f2aec15fcd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271512}] :question/id #uuid "1044f32f-7390-525d-94d0-30c2ecd3e1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141829" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-4{x}^{2}+4x=-2.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}={(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $1$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-4),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1-1 & =-2-1 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c9dab48-f4ec-50d5-997f-66003dcb0d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} {:answer/id #uuid "78463560-21e0-5b25-866c-e90cd7340bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388844} {:answer/id #uuid "b9db4f34-08d3-52b7-b130-1953818d1cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388845} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcc5cac-e58c-525f-92e0-44013ae6b5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8898ba-df5f-5bff-a12b-8e45e358e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} :answer-field/id #uuid "32deee12-64c9-53b5-a191-c9595d854af8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388842}] :question/id #uuid "6cd3a3fd-0ec4-5982-bdba-cd236ee44004" :question/math-academy-id "q-141811" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}+24x=-11.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}+6x+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x+9=(x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+24x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+6x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x) & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[(x+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $36$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+3)^{2}-36 & =-11 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2}-36+36 & =-11+36 \\\\ 4(x+3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |x+3| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a5c631-15ae-5872-945b-175674fabfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506221} {:answer/id #uuid "bd51cecd-6c04-520f-9fa4-54a69b142f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506252} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0938db-92c7-585b-8cbd-31116bc33d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506271} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e520c-9345-5603-92aa-5989d3118aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38d1eab-b846-57b1-8eca-3e79f6301a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506494}] :question/id #uuid "1724c4ea-89ed-51e2-a2bf-8d02f0f074fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-141806" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bb1ee1-44d2-5a8b-a0ff-8a61b2d8e549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd21be66-27be-55af-af71-359acfb6c6dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506507}] :question/id #uuid "cca814ca-cf90-5add-8be7-53a49b278718" :question/math-academy-id "q-218766" :question/problem "Solve the equation $9{x}^{2}-18x=7.$  Write the solutions in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*\n\n$\\,{x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-18x & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides and then dividing by $9$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9+9 & =7+9 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"} :step/id #uuid "4923c949-f86d-5707-8ac7-0bcd1b408f05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184963}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185300 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc56cc55-b423-5e0b-aed0-e07bdb86f874" :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Formula"} :step/id #uuid "3f1ff0dc-f8c4-5e2b-90b2-8f6efe37b016"}] :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Completing the Square" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184433}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078 :topic/id #uuid "7f1179a4-dcfa-50b2-b12b-655dfefa3ff3" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} {:answer/id #uuid "626efd7c-bc12-5b9b-928b-743c558ef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245177} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99c3dc-ae1f-5598-a413-19b87a0bf35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245397} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc842e-e24a-567f-8bf4-a2a2a63772a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245580} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c838f7-516c-56c5-9e1d-df6702452695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} :answer-field/id #uuid "7819a661-bbd8-5b64-a989-336243949373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246755}] :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({k}^{2}-16k+8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-16k+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-56.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}-16k+8) & =-({(k-8)}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =-(k-8)^{2}+56 \\\\  & =56-(k-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7577ab6-8d7f-53ff-bcb3-0e64c5384c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181070} {:answer/id #uuid "34819080-d0db-5bca-af55-258eda27cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181071} {:answer/id #uuid "4a89838e-784e-5f1b-854b-494aa17cff2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181072} {:answer/id #uuid "4e117ad9-9124-5c46-9eb1-e5861219a85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181073} {:answer/id #uuid "7efa19ef-bfb4-510e-963b-9fbf1114bd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 - (x - 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181074}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bec44d47-1b43-556a-b376-a48ca1bd2bca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181069}] :question/id #uuid "05a0ce62-2564-57ca-a483-37c403c564b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140269" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^{2} + 2x + 12$ by completing the square."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} {:answer/id #uuid "9f60c504-aeae-5275-9938-cb7e27e9ec18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270685} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ada0ec-df6d-5400-b1fa-9d1ba5b69efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271041} {:answer/id #uuid "fb114b87-c749-5289-8848-51e528face0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271466} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a8a1e-0990-533c-8797-de1d653da457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} :answer-field/id #uuid "537b2e4e-ba82-5a3e-9315-02cb6492d8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271661}] :question/id #uuid "e832f16e-81bf-5dd5-9dcf-748202928d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140318" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+4x-12)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a0bb28-2f69-527e-9789-2acd61f99dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270603} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ddc0d9-1590-5828-933b-deda6de12aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270707} {:answer/id #uuid "ac58089a-07bf-5122-9205-10bdc8c984fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270807} {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} {:answer/id #uuid "c9803867-897b-5c92-9521-9f0b3409dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0bb4535-ddbc-5c9e-b8f1-ec33a353b4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271859}] :question/id #uuid "3fe4be51-7638-5af1-be56-d66a24129d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-140321" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+10y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5db459-9a1a-5731-8ec2-fd3d7a0c226b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c96ca7-4a6f-5e81-9112-d6132be7b716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290062}] :question/id #uuid "a2c104a3-b59c-55e6-a052-aa1a23a45e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-218604" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+12x+14)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({x}^{2}+12x+14)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f29405-e6ce-5f2a-b069-a560474e1dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f29405-e6ce-5f2a-b069-a560474e1dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289364} :answer-field/id #uuid "362835da-a246-5548-9ab2-ee0a5f8aab8f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b30fee-ae36-5594-be06-c681d75efd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b30fee-ae36-5594-be06-c681d75efd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288829} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cc010e-7157-5281-955e-5c97dbe14652" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290119}] :question/id #uuid "13575951-a185-56a9-b6a8-88b94c3d4b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-218662" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}+16x+1$ by completing the square.\n\n$-{x}^{2}+16x+1=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}-16x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-65.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16x+1 & =-((x-8)^{2}-65) \\\\  & =-(x-8)^{2}+65 \\\\  & =65-(x-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} {:answer/id #uuid "2367ac0c-5a4d-592e-a6c4-bb3ab96af7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500539} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa52ca5-7f84-5524-9e35-b67f97468bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500652} {:answer/id #uuid "76d55117-ac24-5597-977e-26f389735915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500713} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cd2e45-2799-51f7-8b68-c7756837046d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} :answer-field/id #uuid "753b685b-ee64-5d8e-94ea-d970946416c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501069}] :question/id #uuid "5bc52b75-4c42-58c5-bcab-077cbe6faec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80579" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-6x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13498b09-a219-5dfa-9293-f4041b9703a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500508} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4aa913-b332-54d8-be5c-49036736de34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500559} {:answer/id #uuid "78c75f6c-f643-59ba-801f-b5a91dc0bb7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500718} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9ac659-5538-517c-8887-28133c7a46d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6730d9-206d-5f68-9d5b-5aff820cffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501073}] :question/id #uuid "82dc9974-9e37-5f33-ad85-854a2a504fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112299" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{t}^{2}-12t-7$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1542e3-e27b-5f3b-a9cf-d176370aebd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500497} {:answer/id #uuid "702b8582-b9b0-567b-af2b-a8dfa70c095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500695} {:answer/id #uuid "87a9058b-6617-5b7b-a686-12d2470baa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500751} {:answer/id #uuid "9269d8f3-f3eb-5f87-9901-6dc53e2775ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19+(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500768} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320e095-8ba2-55cc-97f8-a79ff22fad04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501078}] :question/id #uuid "e1ed5444-a38b-5b2e-8f5a-4a0e97b37ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-25254" :question/problem "Rewrite $-19-14z-{z}^{2}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({z}^{2}+14z+19)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+14z+19\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+19\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${7}^{2},$ writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+{7}^{2}}_{⏟}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-30\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19-14z-{z}^{2} & =-((z+7)^{2}-30) \\\\  & =-(z+7)^{2}+30 \\\\  & =30-(z+7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} {:answer/id #uuid "2def7528-a392-5c38-a317-849706e41d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565747} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8f5a47-f218-5790-b0cf-2c6bd714ec14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565949} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dc3995-7e49-5218-bd7f-84bc849ae212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565981} {:answer/id #uuid "f44e234d-afc4-503a-ae90-2371906eb7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e21de81-a1b0-5b51-8bf2-2764e594128c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566108}] :question/id #uuid "71c17160-e343-5b78-8eee-e55ba4967a40" :question/math-academy-id "q-140322" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-12x+35)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b6c4f-e84d-5b59-96f6-ec5ff4ca3b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565761} {:answer/id #uuid "3662e5af-46a7-5722-9ccd-5d2228b374fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565764} {:answer/id #uuid "4b141f66-de8e-506c-a57a-d99a3a23fc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-76-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565794} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3c2400-2c72-5f8d-b4b6-ba5f48a46492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ff7a4b-bfcf-57eb-b578-4dd542e44b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566186}] :question/id #uuid "bb8a5938-134a-51c9-838d-3e4010a8ca50" :question/math-academy-id "q-140319" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-8y+60)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fa85ec-5f23-5583-a1e2-3b339c957d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565708} {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8b8549-d167-5182-a05e-094b9fd3a421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565853} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d26802-0604-5ec9-a6dd-63413f57143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565977} {:answer/id #uuid "dbffa828-6ec7-5c64-9988-48bed410abc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} :answer-field/id #uuid "109e1a5c-722e-5d5b-b384-b35a60da59b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566109}] :question/id #uuid "e52c4055-fdc4-580f-9736-b9940edca056" :question/math-academy-id "q-25252" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{a}^{2}-10a+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184116 :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2441e55c-3656-5b9c-9444-a562a3ff3b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269586} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3faa2d-7189-540c-8002-572e91da81bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269984} {:answer/id #uuid "82317f98-eb55-5a2a-84b7-c0fc441aa39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270394} {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} {:answer/id #uuid "f37d3db7-779c-579c-a14f-a3675bd5b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} :answer-field/id #uuid "02be3ebb-ff12-5196-b096-96eac16ef254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509}] :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9{z}^{2}-6z+9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9.$ So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ddff9c-ac54-5bdb-8e05-4c9e80b9afd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329975} {:answer/id #uuid "30d8498f-8b3e-524c-b327-f7f1af56ad01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330381} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ac45d-48a3-5d22-9f35-e862992b052f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e12e2d-9ac1-56f3-b293-e94794a6a02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332078} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171} :answer-field/id #uuid "064cff71-f6ed-52e2-8855-063c335fc1b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332205}] :question/id #uuid "f4b82526-21d1-5906-b903-379b7a33781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52544" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+6y+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} {:answer/id #uuid "67107a8a-2dd9-5dbc-b0a4-79d01366d76a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270186} {:answer/id #uuid "e5de3296-93d1-57f6-a0c1-155f521a694c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271292} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9bacd1-e2fe-51ca-b317-8cb0d98826a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-4)}^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271365} {:answer/id #uuid "efc066ec-cde9-533c-9085-bb96bb129a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(5x+4)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} :answer-field/id #uuid "127abff6-4446-5351-8594-23776f79806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271538}] :question/id #uuid "11a225fd-d96c-5a5e-9031-b51e03cdd249" :question/math-academy-id "q-140532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-40x-60$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0245ee6f-6eed-595d-8f05-de3a8a337d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269288} {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} {:answer/id #uuid "477fcb1e-4945-5111-8193-a1e04fd1813c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269917} {:answer/id #uuid "5bebba35-09ab-5b75-8274-fdce5263c6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270087} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0a43b-11d0-52d9-a265-d5894d01e6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0808a8-74a4-5bb5-83bd-e46fd04336eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271651}] :question/id #uuid "1e4eb76b-1e24-5200-b802-18e77326289b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140536" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311} :answer-field/id #uuid "02af0bbe-51a6-5d5a-8c08-cd4c322fac91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2b726d-3532-55e2-ab3b-c21e3a83a5cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316} :answer-field/id #uuid "61042d02-bc0e-5fb3-8749-0a4f5cca1d61" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939}] :question/id #uuid "4b993c3c-e8bb-5de5-8511-a2d9d6d2b174" :question/math-academy-id "q-218759" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53025447-2eb1-5df5-a00f-6e8c8a103513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319630} {:answer/id #uuid "66db5717-8d6c-56e3-9e6e-8159327ca744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319706} {:answer/id #uuid "6f875b91-463b-5b9e-b642-9334220d3e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319727} {:answer/id 