{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+21\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y-21 & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183944 :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0f7330-a210-5dab-b07f-e27d8a72ece9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500496} {:answer/id #uuid "49d841e1-6a02-5efb-8678-6fe6afc6b67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500614} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae41a75-4ff5-594a-978f-7c64fa1799af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(8x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500758} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc81ce1-b560-5ed7-949e-b540fa0823c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(8x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500760} {:answer/id #uuid "ac757b0b-83df-5393-8914-188485d81214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d841e1-6a02-5efb-8678-6fe6afc6b67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500614} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5323cf-6dd2-553c-91f2-c20ecad02240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183433}] :question/id #uuid "62243cf5-4bf2-5f4d-9d71-de0fec8db876" :question/math-academy-id "q-52541" :question/problem "Rewrite $8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, we take out a factor of $8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x) & =8({(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184805 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441e55e1-aa5d-5f13-99f6-88a5e7c3e36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(3y+1)}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330560} {:answer/id #uuid "565cfed1-3741-5c54-ba4d-d94d6f905a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(y-1)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330722} {:answer/id #uuid "98b21fbc-38a4-5efa-864f-eea4dbdd9c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(y+1)}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331312} {:answer/id #uuid "b2449cbe-6c57-5abd-806e-eda48c58c93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(y+1)}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331525} {:answer/id #uuid "e323633e-2825-5f05-8615-4bb220111eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(y-1)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2449cbe-6c57-5abd-806e-eda48c58c93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(y+1)}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331525} :answer-field/id #uuid "f10cd1b2-f585-5dae-879c-1b936f42bb7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332625}] :question/id #uuid "5b21db9d-e784-5ce2-96a2-6fd7b3008ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-52542" :question/problem "Rewrite $3{y}^{2}+6y+15$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({y}^{2}+2y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 1y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({y}^{2}+2y+5) & =3({(y+1)}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3{(y+1)}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} {:answer/id #uuid "2c113b7e-7f2d-50c2-84be-19bb33799b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269664} {:answer/id #uuid "55abb69f-b764-531c-95eb-eeec736810ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270028} {:answer/id #uuid "d6232d29-c295-5652-a6f9-4789848d3505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271146} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5d1b87-4d59-56d8-ac27-ed5cb43e9203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25399f39-cd1a-5142-83fd-2faed5a58382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269599} :answer-field/id #uuid "286cef5c-d3e8-5bea-859b-e4618279b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271585}] :question/id #uuid "0e4b3efa-abe1-5a91-86fa-e6dadf579120" :question/math-academy-id "q-140375" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}+10x-\\frac{11}{6}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{11}{36}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{36}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}x-\\frac{11}{36}) & =6({(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102a30de-999c-5721-9c7b-063f8071b140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269413} {:answer/id #uuid "23953c7d-cf98-58a8-9973-133a833ff4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269581} {:answer/id #uuid "e4255eb5-9f74-5ac6-9031-ca5e6cbae4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271279} {:answer/id #uuid "e4975d6e-5d51-5f97-bc90-be1042f45ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271284} {:answer/id #uuid "f9957bf7-7006-5741-9d61-53cef2156cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9957bf7-7006-5741-9d61-53cef2156cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271452} :answer-field/id #uuid "02699db9-8f59-5c37-91e1-d12537c34bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271508}] :question/id #uuid "838eecfd-7ac7-5b5a-b69c-54436c56049e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140528" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-12x+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-6x+8) & =2((x-3)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c46ce32-bfb4-59b1-8c45-4b802c060103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c46ce32-bfb4-59b1-8c45-4b802c060103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288123} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4ac515-022f-515e-b805-31a391237daf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548fc6ac-b128-5b5f-a1cb-e95b2acbe9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548fc6ac-b128-5b5f-a1cb-e95b2acbe9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288615} :answer-field/id #uuid "40666057-5be2-580b-9847-e7d022ff0f2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77483c12-fdf3-5046-999c-0c3ee2fffd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77483c12-fdf3-5046-999c-0c3ee2fffd18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288854} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a33f47-6b1e-5c84-b1c2-76b8a9ae9fba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289979}] :question/id #uuid "a16b1eda-c529-5caa-8c46-16ed1020b267" :question/math-academy-id "q-218756" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{p}^{2}-12p+7$ by completing the square.\n\n$6{p}^{2}-12p+7=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(p\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{6}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 1p+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(p-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({p}^{2}-2p+\\frac{7}{6}) & =6({(p-1)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =6{(p-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e78b7b8-004a-5ae1-89b6-a349717f8b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319430} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2ae898-fc32-5ab7-ba22-8edeeac60f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-3)}^{2}-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319620} {:answer/id #uuid "68ceb6c5-7f1c-5888-9e54-6c2610f7034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319713} {:answer/id #uuid "7930b66d-2e5b-5b3e-9ed8-cf8517630210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-3)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319755} {:answer/id #uuid "d453fdfb-2dac-5470-895e-2dec8916b85d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d453fdfb-2dac-5470-895e-2dec8916b85d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320038} :answer-field/id #uuid "f92b56f7-489b-5564-9964-94190dd352b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320694}] :question/id #uuid "0442c281-b5c4-54d4-892e-8d2dfb2d94ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-35081" :question/problem "Rewrite $3{q}^{2}-9q+15$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a3a43a-0340-5e3c-9bcf-b1d4cc0763a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330152} {:answer/id #uuid "4b779a67-3896-55fc-be1f-97694c6c476f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330635} {:answer/id #uuid "af563452-6627-5310-af3e-8f3e9187bdf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331496} {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} {:answer/id #uuid "effab392-c1df-59af-aa5f-e43acece335d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x+1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e8f7ca-397d-5324-b14e-634be47e4ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332374}] :question/id #uuid "0793571e-bec3-5375-a73d-bbdb84782691" :question/math-academy-id "q-52522" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a795cb-9f0e-5939-900e-adafa303f8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330103} {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} {:answer/id #uuid "72076787-7552-58b8-8ad4-00b516cbaf7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "98ee28e9-1737-5e3b-81c5-05141104f588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "f630c36b-13b8-5df3-93c0-7e5b92d1a0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d388fcd-2c84-5c86-86ee-03feb01e614c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301}] :question/id #uuid "38a732d9-8b0e-58f3-ab88-f23eff634c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29685454-9e89-546a-bdb5-50f4294edfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330317} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb811c0-7cf3-5a37-9338-fbc339b8c10b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330768} {:answer/id #uuid "70e2e8a0-02ea-5dc3-96c1-2a23a510b938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330963} {:answer/id #uuid "cd46d12a-92b5-5c2b-80b7-ef1bd3e75c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331770} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa28c1-50ea-591c-b863-be95e01f2d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29685454-9e89-546a-bdb5-50f4294edfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330317} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c22ed9b-d0d8-5857-aa56-4117fcc8f070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332475}] :question/id #uuid "5fbf174c-0450-5828-bb2d-71a05a24426d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52538" :question/problem "Rewrite $5{x}^{2}-20x+25$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, we take out a factor of $5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n5({x}^{2}-4x+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =5((x-2)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4d1d7e-a98a-55e8-bd06-08ce93773259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500548} {:answer/id #uuid "2c63e279-30b2-51d5-b8e5-956913e9730a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500560} {:answer/id #uuid "43ec7ab1-5c7b-5fcc-ab24-6478badcd194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500600} {:answer/id #uuid "980a2775-c11b-5cae-b3d8-18d6a1cfca30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500778} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cb9f0b-5610-5d8c-b091-422fff9bdf3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4d1d7e-a98a-55e8-bd06-08ce93773259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500548} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba3c8f5-6904-5eb8-80c0-90dc9cc21c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501075}] :question/id #uuid "8898e4ea-fd1e-55a2-a1c5-4594ef5b35cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-140372" :question/problem "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+24x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+6x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c81066-fa93-57ad-9c31-169594b16f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566076} :answer-field/id #uuid "321f92c3-450e-5fba-993b-2e46cd9808aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ce5e56-780d-5c88-bed9-f6bd700d42ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565679} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c39176-488f-543b-a2da-3abb77eaa53b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566154} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15719c4e-b7fb-5170-8bb8-2901fc8bc636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565714} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f40a4d7-d407-5619-8448-b04becc5c8a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566164}] :question/id #uuid "5eff477c-d77b-5edc-9e57-fb4474642e96" :question/math-academy-id "q-218758" :question/problem "Rewrite $10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}$ by completing the square.\n\n$10{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{7}{10}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, we take out a factor of $10$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{100}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}x+{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{100}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{10}x+\\frac{7}{100}) & =10({(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{50}) \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+10\\cdot \\frac{3}{50} \\\\  & =10{(x+\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b4d955-924d-5b66-994e-cd8d4afee070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+8)}^{2}+40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565722} {:answer/id #uuid "9fda3e7d-5801-5dc8-9c05-1ae2500e0fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565932} {:answer/id #uuid "afcdb75c-e813-595d-bae0-066b25d66f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565956} {:answer/id #uuid "d752feaf-59fd-5134-932a-9f884eace22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566026} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f4119d-6a4b-5ef7-897f-562be8a71f60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+8)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afcdb75c-e813-595d-bae0-066b25d66f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+4)}^{2}-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565956} :answer-field/id #uuid "952c5bf3-9d6e-5030-8f7f-3c940131b3ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566166}] :question/id #uuid "6fcb379b-2e62-54ea-ab65-5d0b75f0dbcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140530" :question/problem "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+32x+40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+8x+10)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-6\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+8x+10) & =4((x+4)^{2}-6) \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =4{(x+4)}^{2}-24\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2204f3-0b9c-5716-8b12-1daaa8e9c98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565695} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcec967-e7ae-5ddf-bf71-07068fba0434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565797} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} {:answer/id #uuid "abd335e9-aa90-5300-ad70-e9bfcbdab375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(7x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565950} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a8f032-2fca-5906-b4f7-ab9bf75bf249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5aa0-fb14-5496-9591-622a3ce17833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565829} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c32e90-669a-52ff-9868-695c63e890c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566189}] :question/id #uuid "a3f44aac-b56c-541f-849d-0822403501d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-52539" :question/problem "Rewrite $7{x}^{2}-21x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, we take out a factor of $7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n7({x}^{2}-3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7({x}^{2}-3x) & =7({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-7\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =7{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{63}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :topic/title "Completing the Square With Leading Coefficients"} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184993} :activity/id #uuid "c850d730-254f-5cca-b5da-80eca1a9051f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185337 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to complete the square on the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n- \\left(x^2 + 6x+4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 6x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x  + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2+ {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+6x+4) & =-((x+3)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "50b34082-7844-5fd2-aaaf-8d4a7fe58412" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10a00ef6-4086-5bc9-b1f4-a84242b5b363" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} {:answer/id #uuid "626efd7c-bc12-5b9b-928b-743c558ef6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245177} {:answer/id #uuid "7b99c3dc-ae1f-5598-a413-19b87a0bf35c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245397} {:answer/id #uuid "90fc842e-e24a-567f-8bf4-a2a2a63772a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-(k+8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245580} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c838f7-516c-56c5-9e1d-df6702452695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05eadef5-d4bf-541e-bd2e-343c29f59c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56-(k-8)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244361} :answer-field/id #uuid "7819a661-bbd8-5b64-a989-336243949373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246755}] :question/id #uuid "7b217693-bc6b-565e-8cf8-4ff0d3fc9863" :question/math-academy-id "q-112300" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({k}^{2}-16k+8)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-16k+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{k}^{2}-2\\cdot 8k+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(k-8)^{2}-56.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}-16k+8) & =-({(k-8)}^{2}-56) \\\\  & =-(k-8)^{2}+56 \\\\  & =56-(k-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7577ab6-8d7f-53ff-bcb3-0e64c5384c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181070} {:answer/id #uuid "34819080-d0db-5bca-af55-258eda27cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181071} {:answer/id #uuid "4a89838e-784e-5f1b-854b-494aa17cff2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181072} {:answer/id #uuid "4e117ad9-9124-5c46-9eb1-e5861219a85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 - (x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181073} {:answer/id #uuid "7efa19ef-bfb4-510e-963b-9fbf1114bd9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 - (x - 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181074}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bec44d47-1b43-556a-b376-a48ca1bd2bca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181069}] :question/id #uuid "05a0ce62-2564-57ca-a483-37c403c564b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140269" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^{2} + 2x + 12$ by completing the square."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} {:answer/id #uuid "9f60c504-aeae-5275-9938-cb7e27e9ec18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270685} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ada0ec-df6d-5400-b1fa-9d1ba5b69efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271041} {:answer/id #uuid "fb114b87-c749-5289-8848-51e528face0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271466} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2a8a1e-0990-533c-8797-de1d653da457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "059f6a26-1b29-519e-a543-69be7ed1b4d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269318} :answer-field/id #uuid "537b2e4e-ba82-5a3e-9315-02cb6492d8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271661}] :question/id #uuid "e832f16e-81bf-5dd5-9dcf-748202928d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140318" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+4x-12)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x-12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}-16.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+4x-12) & =-({(x+2)}^{2}-16) \\\\  & =-{(x+2)}^{2}+16 \\\\  & =16-(x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a0bb28-2f69-527e-9789-2acd61f99dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270603} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ddc0d9-1590-5828-933b-deda6de12aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270707} {:answer/id #uuid "ac58089a-07bf-5122-9205-10bdc8c984fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270807} {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} {:answer/id #uuid "c9803867-897b-5c92-9521-9f0b3409dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0bb4535-ddbc-5c9e-b8f1-ec33a353b4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271859}] :question/id #uuid "3fe4be51-7638-5af1-be56-d66a24129d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-140321" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+10y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5db459-9a1a-5731-8ec2-fd3d7a0c226b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c96ca7-4a6f-5e81-9112-d6132be7b716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290062}] :question/id #uuid "a2c104a3-b59c-55e6-a052-aa1a23a45e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-218604" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+12x+14)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({x}^{2}+12x+14)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f29405-e6ce-5f2a-b069-a560474e1dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f29405-e6ce-5f2a-b069-a560474e1dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289364} :answer-field/id #uuid "362835da-a246-5548-9ab2-ee0a5f8aab8f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72b30fee-ae36-5594-be06-c681d75efd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72b30fee-ae36-5594-be06-c681d75efd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288829} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cc010e-7157-5281-955e-5c97dbe14652" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290119}] :question/id #uuid "13575951-a185-56a9-b6a8-88b94c3d4b8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-218662" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}+16x+1$ by completing the square.\n\n$-{x}^{2}+16x+1=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}-16x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${8}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-{8}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-8)^{2}-65.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16x+1 & =-((x-8)^{2}-65) \\\\  & =-(x-8)^{2}+65 \\\\  & =65-(x-8)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} {:answer/id #uuid "2367ac0c-5a4d-592e-a6c4-bb3ab96af7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500539} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa52ca5-7f84-5524-9e35-b67f97468bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500652} {:answer/id #uuid "76d55117-ac24-5597-977e-26f389735915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500713} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cd2e45-2799-51f7-8b68-c7756837046d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} :answer-field/id #uuid "753b685b-ee64-5d8e-94ea-d970946416c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501069}] :question/id #uuid "5bc52b75-4c42-58c5-bcab-077cbe6faec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80579" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-6x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13498b09-a219-5dfa-9293-f4041b9703a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500508} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4aa913-b332-54d8-be5c-49036736de34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500559} {:answer/id #uuid "78c75f6c-f643-59ba-801f-b5a91dc0bb7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500718} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9ac659-5538-517c-8887-28133c7a46d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6730d9-206d-5f68-9d5b-5aff820cffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501073}] :question/id #uuid "82dc9974-9e37-5f33-ad85-854a2a504fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112299" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{t}^{2}-12t-7$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1542e3-e27b-5f3b-a9cf-d176370aebd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500497} {:answer/id #uuid "702b8582-b9b0-567b-af2b-a8dfa70c095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500695} {:answer/id #uuid "87a9058b-6617-5b7b-a686-12d2470baa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500751} {:answer/id #uuid "9269d8f3-f3eb-5f87-9901-6dc53e2775ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19+(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500768} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320e095-8ba2-55cc-97f8-a79ff22fad04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501078}] :question/id #uuid "e1ed5444-a38b-5b2e-8f5a-4a0e97b37ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-25254" :question/problem "Rewrite $-19-14z-{z}^{2}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({z}^{2}+14z+19)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+14z+19\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+19\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${7}^{2},$ writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+{7}^{2}}_{⏟}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-30\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19-14z-{z}^{2} & =-((z+7)^{2}-30) \\\\  & =-(z+7)^{2}+30 \\\\  & =30-(z+7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} {:answer/id #uuid "2def7528-a392-5c38-a317-849706e41d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565747} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8f5a47-f218-5790-b0cf-2c6bd714ec14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565949} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dc3995-7e49-5218-bd7f-84bc849ae212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565981} {:answer/id #uuid "f44e234d-afc4-503a-ae90-2371906eb7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35-(x+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12fd1b63-f3c4-5f58-9b24-c3b8e327902e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565710} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e21de81-a1b0-5b51-8bf2-2764e594128c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566108}] :question/id #uuid "71c17160-e343-5b78-8eee-e55ba4967a40" :question/math-academy-id "q-140322" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-12x+35)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-12x+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+35\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $35$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+35\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+35) & =-({(x-6)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-{(x-6)}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-(x-6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354b6c4f-e84d-5b59-96f6-ec5ff4ca3b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565761} {:answer/id #uuid "3662e5af-46a7-5722-9ccd-5d2228b374fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565764} {:answer/id #uuid "4b141f66-de8e-506c-a57a-d99a3a23fc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-76-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565794} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3c2400-2c72-5f8d-b4b6-ba5f48a46492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2f33409-e27b-5ceb-ba94-f59981011277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-44-(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565962} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ff7a4b-bfcf-57eb-b578-4dd542e44b32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566186}] :question/id #uuid "bb8a5938-134a-51c9-838d-3e4010a8ca50" :question/math-academy-id "q-140319" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-8y+60)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-8y+60\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+60\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $60$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+60\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+44.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-8y+60) & =-({(y-4)}^{2}+44) \\\\  & =-{(y-4)}^{2}-44 \\\\  & =-44-(y-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fa85ec-5f23-5583-a1e2-3b339c957d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565708} {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8b8549-d167-5182-a05e-094b9fd3a421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565853} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d26802-0604-5ec9-a6dd-63413f57143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(a-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565977} {:answer/id #uuid "dbffa828-6ec7-5c64-9988-48bed410abc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d0d9be-3816-5756-9034-3199caae3743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41-(a+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565731} :answer-field/id #uuid "109e1a5c-722e-5d5b-b384-b35a60da59b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566109}] :question/id #uuid "e52c4055-fdc4-580f-9736-b9940edca056" :question/math-academy-id "q-25252" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{a}^{2}-10a+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({a}^{2}+10a-16)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+10a-16\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a-16\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{a}^{2}+2\\cdot 5a+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}-16\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(a+5)^{2}-41\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-10a-{a}^{2} & =-((a+5)^{2}-41) \\\\  & =-(a+5)^{2}+41 \\\\  & =41-(a+5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"} :step/id #uuid "65a066ee-807a-50d9-8957-f31276799bef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185198}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184116 :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2441e55c-3656-5b9c-9444-a562a3ff3b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269586} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3faa2d-7189-540c-8002-572e91da81bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269984} {:answer/id #uuid "82317f98-eb55-5a2a-84b7-c0fc441aa39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270394} {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} {:answer/id #uuid "f37d3db7-779c-579c-a14f-a3675bd5b658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca440db9-063c-5ec8-89e2-7e47d8b1e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271044} :answer-field/id #uuid "02be3ebb-ff12-5196-b096-96eac16ef254" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509}] :question/id #uuid "2495fc0b-8e76-56e1-bf72-c223c52c4fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-140378" :question/problem "Rewrite $-9{z}^{2}-6z+9$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-9.$ So, we take out a factor of $-9$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9({z}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}z-1) & =-9({(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot (-\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =-9{(z+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ddff9c-ac54-5bdb-8e05-4c9e80b9afd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329975} {:answer/id #uuid "30d8498f-8b3e-524c-b327-f7f1af56ad01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330381} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ac45d-48a3-5d22-9f35-e862992b052f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y+1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e12e2d-9ac1-56f3-b293-e94794a6a02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332078} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb7f176-7c6f-5b6e-950c-ed129b00ef62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332171} :answer-field/id #uuid "064cff71-f6ed-52e2-8855-063c335fc1b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332205}] :question/id #uuid "f4b82526-21d1-5906-b903-379b7a33781d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52544" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+6y+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{3}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-2y-\\frac{1}{3}) & =-3({(y-1)}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} {:answer/id #uuid "67107a8a-2dd9-5dbc-b0a4-79d01366d76a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270186} {:answer/id #uuid "e5de3296-93d1-57f6-a0c1-155f521a694c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271292} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9bacd1-e2fe-51ca-b317-8cb0d98826a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-4)}^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271365} {:answer/id #uuid "efc066ec-cde9-533c-9085-bb96bb129a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(5x+4)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5e40a0-bb1b-5159-95df-8a81f60527ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269676} :answer-field/id #uuid "127abff6-4446-5351-8594-23776f79806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271538}] :question/id #uuid "11a225fd-d96c-5a5e-9031-b51e03cdd249" :question/math-academy-id "q-140532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-40x-60$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+8x+12)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+12\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+12\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $12$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+12\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}-4\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+8x+12) & =-5((x+4)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-5{(x+4)}^{2}+20\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0245ee6f-6eed-595d-8f05-de3a8a337d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269288} {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} {:answer/id #uuid "477fcb1e-4945-5111-8193-a1e04fd1813c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269917} {:answer/id #uuid "5bebba35-09ab-5b75-8274-fdce5263c6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270087} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0a43b-11d0-52d9-a265-d5894d01e6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0808a8-74a4-5bb5-83bd-e46fd04336eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271651}] :question/id #uuid "1e4eb76b-1e24-5200-b802-18e77326289b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140536" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14db59a-5903-5eb2-a881-001d6a5d5e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289311} :answer-field/id #uuid "02af0bbe-51a6-5d5a-8c08-cd4c322fac91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1f1c3a8-e3fd-557c-98d8-6af928d2c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289113} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2b726d-3532-55e2-ab3b-c21e3a83a5cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2207dc9-eda4-56d0-ba73-d6dcca1b8315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289316} :answer-field/id #uuid "61042d02-bc0e-5fb3-8749-0a4f5cca1d61" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289939}] :question/id #uuid "4b993c3c-e8bb-5de5-8511-a2d9d6d2b174" :question/math-academy-id "q-218759" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}+32x-29$ by completing the square.\n\n$-8{x}^{2}+32x-29=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{29}{8}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}-\\frac{29}{8}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{29}{8}) & =-8({(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(x-2)}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3-8{(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53025447-2eb1-5df5-a00f-6e8c8a103513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{89}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319630} {:answer/id #uuid "66db5717-8d6c-56e3-9e6e-8159327ca744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{67}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319706} {:answer/id #uuid "6f875b91-463b-5b9e-b642-9334220d3e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{49}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319727} {:answer/id #uuid "78093d05-d766-51e5-bfbd-15a4b1ad83e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319753} {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b538e599-0c29-5367-95db-1d80a51d71e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319948} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1dc974-537c-5a19-8b0e-de4ef3bf427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320478}] :question/id #uuid "8b2f4df1-3e4e-5541-bb2f-d2b7890aaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-35079" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3331d0b6-f01e-59e2-bb75-e67d4ef740a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(7x+1)}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500577} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd5cdd8-8878-58c6-9eaf-b9cafe08a766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500588} {:answer/id #uuid "4798e25d-373c-5c45-8f37-74c1becad3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x-1)}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500609} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3d105b-231e-5660-aea4-11b4dd11aa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500900} {:answer/id #uuid "d57e2a5d-9ff9-5289-b4aa-35eb7211d36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x-1)}^{2}-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd5cdd8-8878-58c6-9eaf-b9cafe08a766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500588} :answer-field/id #uuid "a568522c-d69a-5ea7-b2da-992338459b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501094}] :question/id #uuid "07e09125-f910-5338-a71f-7baf6d758234" :question/math-academy-id "q-140376" :question/problem "Rewrite $-7{x}^{2}-14x+7$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-7.$ So, we take out a factor of $-7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-7({x}^{2}+2x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({x}^{2}+2x-1) & =-7({(x+1)}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+(-7)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc5889c-f956-5805-828b-a07b04225e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500725} {:answer/id #uuid "805a1efe-9356-5170-a269-8ed6c903aa17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500733} {:answer/id #uuid "d93ffed7-ac55-5ecb-a0d9-3189b1b4cd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500929} {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} {:answer/id #uuid "f499ebac-94ab-572a-a984-e07f21d31ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} :answer-field/id #uuid "28822793-1aa6-5555-91d5-42b23da22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501031}] :question/id #uuid "517afe53-217c-5ede-9811-b9bf164398f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140380" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d83943b-fa05-5a32-815b-4e9cc7e9a241" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7cf4a-4ced-579f-b3b1-a8420309518b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7e8649-8e07-5c84-b1f7-d22b5da0ebf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501063}] :question/id #uuid "badd637f-464d-556b-9b5c-ae357a07836a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218760" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260cd36f-3cd1-5e6e-a08e-7d3ca9370c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500542} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba08eda-a7a0-539e-92cd-414b03d79077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500722} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1e521-d9dc-5058-a940-b2513a2a3010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500832} {:answer/id #uuid "b17792ba-6da9-5712-933a-ecb6ba3164b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500837} {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e4a4bf-3e09-5621-be81-4a8b258251af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501120}] :question/id #uuid "fc2b2629-e323-5ab5-9be3-1b12132dec56" :question/math-academy-id "q-140379" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468992e9-ad98-5e6d-b726-dd3130a61cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520921} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd828f8-42ce-5777-935d-cf7d0ddd6df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521057} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a8bed-d21d-52be-9870-71f5031347b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521116} {:answer/id #uuid "c85bb9fd-5526-511a-8a6a-c2514cf0251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521212} {:answer/id #uuid "d5002f69-b154-569c-9f75-ac522a011347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c85bb9fd-5526-511a-8a6a-c2514cf0251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521212} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57e8dee-3a1b-5eb0-a0a9-8388e98ff9a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521417}] :question/id #uuid "20662b40-9142-5228-b622-edf8a16af72c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140476" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{z}^{2}-12z-\\frac{7}{2}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{16}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}) & =-8({(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b2d8e5-9b5c-5216-aeff-851b31557845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520812} {:answer/id #uuid "7c027f5d-f2be-54c0-ba10-816c2c3c2421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521046} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc48979-59bf-5cf0-ab38-814855efe50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521084} {:answer/id #uuid "d260d6cd-2693-5fac-b2ee-6098a1ef0f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(2x+3)}^{2}+17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521236} {:answer/id #uuid "f242b273-5aa7-5a27-95c1-4f47737537b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c027f5d-f2be-54c0-ba10-816c2c3c2421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521046} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece1c1a9-e6aa-5e0f-8f5d-a9fac0d03848" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521446}] :question/id #uuid "db65568c-c0a4-5de2-8240-a716b187636f" :question/math-academy-id "q-25250" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x+4$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x-2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x-2) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383a1a6d-35ea-5270-9184-75c2682d7396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565769} {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d2b6a-5266-5c53-ac87-e02a1827047e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{10}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565802} {:answer/id #uuid "c97303ff-3ba1-59f2-bae9-fcada6af4564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566004} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d06a-0da6-5642-842f-83c69a159e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44526162-1855-508d-ad71-02db10f1e1b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565788} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fbd290-f3b1-5227-bd0d-a92509c57f4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566144}] :question/id #uuid "71daf213-45ae-576f-97ae-9ffbff2f758a" :question/math-academy-id "q-52543" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b3902d-1015-53f0-995d-a66e100771ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(4x-1)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565709} {:answer/id #uuid "307076da-9752-5562-860a-d357b7f3f900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(4x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565754} {:answer/id #uuid "3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565774} {:answer/id #uuid "86e1cc55-8aa1-5b03-a1af-07dc83b72500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565889} {:answer/id #uuid "b561b8c7-15f6-5cfb-b205-0138ec744c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b842baa-f6f2-52b1-8460-09b16f0bcd80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565774} :answer-field/id #uuid "167b595a-7f5f-59ff-991b-3067a90e82fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566114}] :question/id #uuid "d53c2052-a67a-5988-8f1a-4c9a720c7a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-52532" :question/problem "Rewrite $-4{x}^{2}+8x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, we take out a factor of $-4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-4({x}^{2}-2x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-1)}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}^{2}-2x) & =-4({(x-1)}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-4{(x-1)}^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "7419895d-db2c-5ab3-a035-d7b22a41bccd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183943 :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 -7x +13)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  +\\dfrac{3}4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-13 & =-({(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0710913b-7399-5b9e-a5a9-f4627344dd74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565688} {:answer/id #uuid "0d6b0b59-5f4d-58eb-aec7-42d212af1618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565700} {:answer/id #uuid "6969fc79-b38b-551a-bfdf-07495efdaf43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565842} {:answer/id #uuid "91d492cf-1c56-56df-bd04-4aabaf69aec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31-{(y+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565914} {:answer/id #uuid "fc78b4ea-4ddc-5ed1-8637-95f6e755fa4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(y+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d6b0b59-5f4d-58eb-aec7-42d212af1618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565700} :answer-field/id #uuid "322303e8-364d-5afe-acc4-d76d39051ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181075}] :question/id #uuid "8f29e3f2-9a97-5ae1-80ba-f81c4f7c0fb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140331" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}-5y+6)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+6\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+6\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $6$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}-5y+6) & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057bf8b9-f4be-51c0-a4d1-d57b29da8233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565683} {:answer/id #uuid "61f2ea1d-2d8b-55fa-8520-17155db3eebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{(x+2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565832} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfed3f5-06e5-5e82-b0a9-731fa897dd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566038} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f36cd8-e603-55a8-9ec8-ae98cefb08df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566051} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8880bf-ffee-5976-8095-806532a97526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8880bf-ffee-5976-8095-806532a97526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566098} :answer-field/id #uuid "8091fb4f-3520-5238-a1fe-8deaeb6d643a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186181087}] :question/id #uuid "67f49a8a-532a-55d5-814b-4932396dbbeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140406" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-x-3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+x+3)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+3\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-x-3 & =-({(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "071c98e7-f045-5699-97e6-8ca98ff4c425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{4}-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269336} {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} {:answer/id #uuid "92214c5a-0fb3-5f9c-b2fd-8c3a954013bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270546} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f6b651-8575-5bc8-a027-fe8e7ce11c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270861} {:answer/id #uuid "d730e81c-c1fd-5d3a-9ed2-255c3dad533b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b6551d7-ff9d-5fcb-9ba8-47eb76d9737a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269367} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e101ba8-4e79-5217-9a66-13f1e2c2b19e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271788}] :question/id #uuid "5f049fbc-7296-56e3-bb5f-3d51d2204d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140330" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+7x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+7x+10) & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00259cbe-d8e5-5392-8381-04524c3d0b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-{(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269259} {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab4b2b-b9b2-5933-a43e-16332e47f135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270066} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccf35f0-4836-5526-b487-39bc207dbcf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270233} {:answer/id #uuid "b357bb45-c3bf-5ad2-ab72-033565c17bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} :answer-field/id #uuid "66fc580c-7644-50ba-93bc-29818b02080f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271693}] :question/id #uuid "793139fd-bc0b-52b9-b28f-1f9a809139b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140332" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-x-2)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0b9d03-98ee-5f65-8899-eb0ca3293f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269682} {:answer/id #uuid "51675449-e3ed-5d32-a5a1-dc181d745b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{161}{4}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269997} {:answer/id #uuid "64001e55-d244-54a8-918a-a1c0059b44a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270154} {:answer/id #uuid "88d86ace-1240-59e6-93cf-c53adca2f146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answe